const setup = `CREATE TABLE version_demo (
  product_id integer PRIMARY KEY,
  available integer NOT NULL
) WITH (fillfactor = 70);
INSERT INTO version_demo (product_id, available) VALUES (1, 3);`;
const inspect = `SELECT product_id, available,
       ctid::text, xmin::text, xmax::text
FROM version_demo WHERE product_id = 1;`;
const snapshot = `SELECT pg_current_snapshot();
-- One observed snapshot: 784:786:784
-- Lower bound : upper bound : IDs still active at capture`;
const heap = `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pageinspect;
SELECT lp, lp_flags, t_xmin, t_xmax, t_ctid,
       (heap_tuple_infomask_flags(t_infomask, t_infomask2)).raw_flags
FROM heap_page_items(get_raw_page('version_demo', 0))
ORDER BY lp;`;
const activity = `SELECT pid, application_name, state,
       now() - xact_start AS transaction_duration,
       backend_xmin, age(backend_xmin) AS snapshot_xid_age,
       wait_event_type, wait_event
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE datname = current_database()
  AND backend_xmin IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY age(backend_xmin) DESC;`;
const tables = `SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup,
       n_tup_upd, n_tup_hot_upd,
       last_vacuum, last_autovacuum,
       vacuum_count, autovacuum_count
FROM pg_stat_user_tables
WHERE relname = 'version_demo';

SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid) AS oldest_unfrozen_xid_age
FROM pg_database ORDER BY age(datfrozenxid) DESC;`;
const plan = `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)
SELECT product_id, available FROM pg_visibility_products
WHERE product_id <= 5 ORDER BY product_id;`;
---
<Base title="Row versions, transactions, and vacuum" description="Derive snapshot visibility, follow a HOT chain through cleanup, and distinguish retained versions, visibility maps and transaction-ID freezing.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">PostgreSQL</p><h1>Row versions, transactions, and vacuum</h1><TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18" /></header>
<ArticleBody>
  <p>A PostgreSQL table can hold several physical versions of one logical record. Updating a product’s stock from three units to one creates a replacement version; it does not immediately erase the earlier value. A report already reading the database may still need three, while a new request needs one.</p>
  <p>The interesting part is how PostgreSQL makes those two answers coexist. It must decide which version each reader can see, preserve a route from an index to that version, and eventually establish that the earlier bytes are safe to reuse. These decisions connect transaction isolation to the everyday work of vacuum.</p>
  <p>We will follow one stock update through those decisions. A real PostgreSQL 18.6 experiment supplies the row locations, snapshot and cleanup states below. The browser lessons let you inspect a changed version, test snapshot membership and watch heap fetches change. Holding independent transactions open at the same time requires the two-connection server exercise.</p>

  <section id="versions"><h2>What an update leaves behind</h2>
   <p>First, separate the application’s identity from its storage. Our application calls this record product 1. PostgreSQL puts a version of that record on a heap page, at an item slot within the page. Its <code>ctid</code> exposes that location. In this small example the first version is at <code>(0,1)</code>: page zero, slot one.</p>
   <Sql code={setup} label="Create a separate table in a disposable PostgreSQL database" />
   <p>Run this setup once, outside a transaction, on a disposable server database. The lower fillfactor leaves page room for later updates; it makes our physical example easier to reproduce, rather than prescribing a production setting. An ordinary query can inspect system columns beside the stored data:</p>
   <Sql code={inspect} label="Inspect the version visible to this query" />
   <p><code>xmin</code> identifies the transaction that created this version. When another transaction updates it, the earlier version’s header records information about that replacement in <code>xmax</code>, and the new version gets its own creator ID. The application’s product ID remains one. The physical location and version metadata change.</p>
   <p>In our executed run, transaction 783 inserted the initial record. Transaction 784 then updated available stock to one. The heap contained these two versions after 784 committed:</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Two physical stock versions"><table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Product</th><th>Available</th><th>Created by</th><th>Replaced by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(0,1)</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>783</td><td>784</td></tr><tr><td>(0,2)</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>784</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>These are physical observations, not the two rows returned by a normal SELECT. The executor checks visibility before returning data, so one query normally sees the version appropriate to its snapshot. Changing <code>ctid</code> is consequently a poor application identity strategy: an update can move a version, and maintenance can later reuse its old location.</p>
   <p>There is an important limit to reading these columns by eye. A nonzero <code>xmax</code> does not necessarily mean a row has been deleted. Row locking also uses that header information. We verified a visible record with nonzero <code>xmax</code> while another connection held <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code>; its tuple flags identified a lock, not a deletion. Multiple lockers can require MultiXact metadata. PostgreSQL interprets IDs together with flags and transaction status. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-system-columns.html">system-column reference</a> describes what the exposed fields mean.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="snapshot"><h2>Snapshot visibility</h2>
   <p>A snapshot records a boundary in transaction activity. It needs more than “all transaction IDs below this number,” because transactions can finish out of order. A transaction that started earlier may still be writing when a later one commits.</p>
   <p>Our report took its snapshot while writer 784 was still active. Another transaction, 785, had already committed. PostgreSQL reported the snapshot as <code>784:786:784</code>. The three parts are a lower boundary, an upper boundary and the IDs still in progress between them. Below 784, no transaction remained in progress at capture. IDs at or above 786 were beyond the snapshot’s upper boundary. Between those boundaries, 784 was explicitly recorded as active.</p>
   <Sql code={snapshot} label="Read the snapshot on the report's connection" />
   <p>Those snapshot boundaries are also called xmin and xmax. They are fields of the snapshot, with a different job from the creator and replacement metadata in an individual tuple. The matching names can obscure that distinction when inspecting both.</p>
   <p>To decide whether the report can see our earlier version, consider its creation and replacement separately. Creator 783 committed before the snapshot, so its creation is eligible. Replacer 784 was still active when the snapshot was taken, so its replacement is excluded. The report therefore sees the earlier value, three. The new version has creator 784, whose change is excluded, so the report does not see one.</p>
   <VersionStorage kind="snapshot" />
   <p>Committing 784 does not rewrite the report’s saved snapshot. Its active-ID list still says that 784 was in progress at capture. A new reader taking a snapshot after that commit can accept 784’s changes: the earlier version has been replaced and the new version is visible. The two answers follow from the same physical records.</p>
   <p>This also explains why comparing a row’s creator ID to the largest committed ID would fail. Transaction 785 committed first, yet 784 must remain excluded from this report. An aborted creator presents another problem for a number-only rule: being old enough does not make an aborted insertion visible.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-snapshot-membership">snapshot membership lesson</a> lets you vary the boundaries and active list with <code>pg_visible_in_snapshot</code>. That function answers whether a transaction ID belongs before a snapshot’s boundary. It does not consult the commit status of an arbitrary ID to establish complete tuple visibility. Our explanation covers ordinary committed or in-progress transactions; PostgreSQL also handles a transaction’s own writes, subtransactions and the header states introduced above. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-info.html#FUNCTIONS-PG-SNAPSHOT">snapshot functions</a> document their scope.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="reader"><h2>Long-lived snapshots and vacuum</h2>
   <p>To reproduce the retained version, open two connections to the disposable database containing <code>version_demo</code>. A is a report; B is a stock writer. Keep both connected and send commands in this order. Each cell belongs to its labelled connection. Exact transaction IDs will differ from the earlier run.</p>
   <p>If you also want to inspect the physical versions, pause after step 5 and run the page-inspection query below on B while A remains open. Repeat that inspection after step 6. Completing the whole schedule first would miss the retained old payload. To repeat the trial, finish both transactions, drop this disposable <code>version_demo</code> table and run its setup again.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Report and writer transaction schedule"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>A · report</th><th>B · writer</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Wait</td><td><code>BEGIN;</code><br /><code>UPDATE version_demo SET available=1 WHERE product_id=1;</code></td><td>B creates a replacement but has not committed</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;</code><br /><code>SELECT available FROM version_demo WHERE product_id=1;</code></td><td>Leave transaction open</td><td>A reads 3 and establishes its snapshot</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Leave transaction open</td><td><code>COMMIT;</code></td><td>B’s change becomes visible to new snapshots</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>SELECT available FROM version_demo WHERE product_id=1;</code></td><td><code>SELECT available FROM version_demo WHERE product_id=1;</code></td><td>A reads 3; B reads 1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Leave transaction open</td><td><code>VACUUM (VERBOSE) version_demo;</code></td><td>The old version remains needed</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>COMMIT;</code></td><td>After A commits, repeat VACUUM</td><td>The old snapshot no longer prevents cleanup</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>The report’s ordinary SELECT does not wait for B’s row update: it can read the earlier version. Other lock conflicts can still make readers wait, including a table rewrite or schema operation. MVCC does not remove PostgreSQL’s locking system.</p>
   <p>Repeatable Read establishes this stable snapshot at the first ordinary statement, rather than at BEGIN alone. Under Read Committed, each ordinary SELECT takes a fresh snapshot. Replacing the isolation level in this schedule would therefore let A’s second SELECT see one. A single long-running Read Committed query can still retain its own snapshot for the query’s duration.</p>
   <p>In the executed experiment, the first vacuum reported two remaining tuples, including one “dead but not yet removable.” After A committed, the next vacuum removed one tuple. We used an extra short-lived connection to allocate transaction 785 before capturing the illustrated snapshot; the two-connection schedule produces the same retained-version outcome without requiring those exact numbers.</p>
   <p>For a physical inspection, the server’s <code>pageinspect</code> extension exposes the heap page. Installing and using these inspection functions needs suitable privileges. Run this only against your disposable example; it bypasses normal query visibility and is not an application query.</p>
   <Sql code={heap} label="Inspect physical tuple headers in the disposable server example" />
   <p>Before the report ends, slots one and two both contain normal tuple payloads. The earlier tuple points toward the replacement at <code>(0,2)</code>. After cleanup, the first slot changes its role. Understanding that change requires following the index as well as the heap.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="hot"><h2>HOT updates and index references</h2>
   <p>The primary-key B-tree holds product 1’s key and a reference to heap location <code>(0,1)</code>. Our update changes available stock, which this index does not store. There is also room for the replacement on the same heap page. PostgreSQL can use a <strong>heap-only tuple update</strong>, usually called HOT.</p>
   <p>Instead of adding an ordinary index entry for the new version, HOT lets the existing entry reach a chain on the heap page. The earlier version is marked as HOT-updated and links to the replacement. That replacement is marked heap-only: it does not need its own entry in this index. A reader follows the chain and chooses its visible member.</p>
   <VersionStorage kind="hot" />
   <p>Once no relevant snapshot needs the earlier version, page pruning can remove its payload. The original item slot becomes a redirect to the surviving version. The index still points to slot one; that slot now redirects it to slot two. Our heap inspection observed precisely this change, while the B-tree inspection still showed its one reference to <code>(0,1)</code>.</p>
   <p>This is why “vacuum deletes the old row” is an incomplete storage explanation. PostgreSQL must also preserve access paths. With a HOT chain, it can reclaim old payload bytes without making the index lose the current record. Page pruning can happen during ordinary page access as well as vacuum; vacuum coordinates broader cleanup, including dead entries in indexes where needed.</p>
   <p>Now add a covering index that includes stock:</p>
   <Sql code="CREATE INDEX version_demo_cover ON version_demo (product_id) INCLUDE (available);" label="A covering index changes which updates can be HOT" />
   <p>Future changes to <code>available</code> change information stored in this index, even though stock is not its ordered search key. HOT is no longer eligible for that update. In a separate fresh fixture with the covering index present before the update, we observed two index entries, one for each version’s location, instead of the single entry used by the HOT case.</p>
   <p>HOT eligibility depends on every relevant index, not just the index the last SELECT used. It requires page room and unchanged columns referenced by non-summarising indexes. BRIN is a summarising method and has different maintenance behaviour here. Expression indexes and included columns therefore deserve attention when a table’s HOT rate changes after an index deployment.</p>
   <p>A lower fillfactor reserves more room on initially filled heap pages. It can help an otherwise eligible replacement stay on its original page, at the cost of a less densely packed table. It cannot make a changed included column eligible. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-hot.html">HOT documentation</a> connects these conditions to the update counters available for measurement.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="visibility"><h2>Index-only scans and the visibility map</h2>
   <p>The covering index introduces a useful tension. It stores every value needed for a product-ID-and-stock query, but those values alone do not establish whether their row version is visible. Ordinarily that check needs the heap tuple’s metadata. PostgreSQL’s visibility map provides a shortcut for pages whose tuples are already known visible to all transactions.</p>
   <p>The map stores two bits per heap page: <strong>all-visible</strong> and <strong>all-frozen</strong>. For an index-only scan, all-visible is the useful bit. When it is set, the executor can trust that page’s visibility without fetching the heap page for each index entry. A clear bit means PostgreSQL cannot use that shortcut; it does not mean every row on the page is invisible.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-visibility">visibility and heap-fetch lesson</a> creates a separate table with 2,000 products and a covering index. Follow its setup, run vacuum outside a transaction, then inspect the first five products:</p>
   <Sql code="VACUUM (ANALYZE) pg_visibility_products;" label="Maintain the lesson table after its initial load" />
   <Sql code={plan} label="Observe whether the covering query still needs heap visits" />
   <p>The verified initial plan is an Index Only Scan with five returned rows and zero heap fetches. Update product 1 to one available unit and repeat the query. The plan still says Index Only Scan, but it now reports heap fetches. A modification cleared the page’s visibility shortcut. The plan’s name describes an access method; the heap-fetch counter describes how much checking this execution needed.</p>
   <p>Vacuum again after the update commits, with no old reader retained in this lesson, then repeat the plan. Heap fetches return to zero. Stock remains one, three, three, three, three. Vacuum changed the page’s eligibility for the shortcut, not the business result.</p>
   <p>This makes a covering index workload-dependent in two ways. Including a frequently changed column may prevent HOT updates, and frequent page changes may repeatedly clear all-visible bits. The index can still help, but “contains all selected columns” does not by itself promise fewer heap visits. Follow both the write cost and the observed read path. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html">index-only scan explanation</a> develops this relationship.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="freezing"><h2>Why old transaction IDs need freezing</h2>
   <p>Cleanup is not vacuum’s only responsibility. Transaction IDs stored in tuple headers have a finite 32-bit space. PostgreSQL compares them using a circular ordering: IDs roughly within the next half of that space count as ahead, and those within the preceding half count as behind. If an ancient creator ID survived untreated while enough new IDs were allocated, that ordering could eventually make it look like a future transaction.</p>
   <p><strong>Freezing</strong> records that a sufficiently old, committed creator should be treated as permanently old. Its visibility no longer depends on comparing that creator ID with new transactions. This lets a table retain old records safely as transaction IDs advance elsewhere in the cluster.</p>
   <p>Modern PostgreSQL can mark the tuple’s creator frozen in its header flags while retaining the original numerical <code>xmin</code> for inspection. Our <code>VACUUM (FREEZE) version_demo</code> experiment left the surviving tuple’s creator value at 784 while its flags marked the creator frozen. Looking only for a rewritten number would miss the result. <code>VACUUM FREEZE</code> here makes a tiny lab state observable; it is not a recommendation to run it manually on every busy table.</p>
   <p>The visibility map’s all-frozen bit tells vacuum that a page no longer contains transaction-ID work requiring freezing. All-visible and all-frozen are related but different: in our experiment, cleanup first made the page all-visible; the later freeze operation also made it all-frozen. A page can qualify for an index-only read while still needing future freezing work.</p>
   <p>A static table still needs this protection. Its rows may never change, while transactions throughout the cluster continue consuming IDs. Measure ID age with PostgreSQL’s age functions, rather than subtracting numbers or converting calendar days directly into a safe transaction budget. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-id.html">transaction-ID description</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html#VACUUM-FOR-WRAPAROUND">wraparound-prevention guidance</a> explain the representation and maintenance thresholds.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="maintenance"><h2>Diagnosing vacuum and retained versions</h2>
   <p>Suppose a frequently updated table grows and queries perform more heap work. There are at least two different questions: are old versions eligible for removal, and can maintenance process eligible work fast enough? Increasing vacuum effort does not solve the first if a snapshot still needs the versions.</p>
   <p>Begin with transaction ownership and retained snapshots. This query is for an operator with permission to inspect the relevant sessions; monitoring access can restrict what other users see.</p>
   <Sql code={activity} label="Look for sessions retaining old snapshot boundaries" />
   <p>In our experiment, the report appeared as <code>idle in transaction</code>, with backend xmin 784 and a client-read wait. It was waiting for its application to send another command while retaining the snapshot. The wall-clock transaction duration and the ID age answer different questions: how long the transaction has existed, and how much transaction-ID activity has happened beyond its boundary.</p>
   <p>The oldest listed session deserves investigation, not automatic termination. Identify the caller and whether its work can safely finish or be retried. A forgotten transaction in a connection pool may need a missing commit or rollback fixed in the application. An intentional long report may need a shorter transaction, a separate reporting arrangement, or an accepted retention budget. An idle-in-transaction timeout can bound abandoned sessions, but its termination and retry behaviour belongs in the application design.</p>
   <p>This query is a starting point, not a complete list of retention sources. Prepared transactions and replication-related horizons also matter. Physical standby feedback can preserve versions on the primary so standby queries can continue; that moves a maintenance cost across servers. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/">replication chapter</a> follows that tradeoff alongside WAL retention.</p>
   <p>Next compare the table’s update and maintenance history:</p>
   <Sql code={tables} label="Inspect estimated tuple counts, HOT updates and transaction-ID age" />
   <p><code>n_dead_tup</code> is an estimate, not an exact inventory of removable bytes. The update counters are cumulative since their reset, so compare changes over an interval when judging HOT behaviour. A low HOT proportion can follow an indexed-column change or a lack of page room. A recent vacuum timestamp says maintenance ran; it does not prove all dead versions were removable.</p>
   <p>If versions are eligible but accumulate faster than vacuum processes them, examine how often autovacuum starts on this table, how much work each run faces, and the resources available while foreground queries run. A large table’s percentage-based threshold can allow substantial change before triggering a run; table-specific thresholds may make smaller, more regular passes useful. Cost limits and delays affect how aggressively maintenance competes for I/O. Choose values from the measured workload, then observe whether runs catch up.</p>
   <p>Ordinary vacuum usually leaves the table file at its existing size and makes space reusable inside it. It can return empty pages at the file’s end under suitable conditions, but it does not repack every surviving row. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites the table and takes an exclusive table lock. A stable file size after successful ordinary vacuum is therefore compatible with useful work: the next updates may reuse the reclaimed room.</p>
   <p>Keep transaction-ID age in the investigation even if disk usage looks healthy. PostgreSQL can trigger anti-wraparound maintenance independently of the usual dead-tuple threshold. Disabling autovacuum to quiet a busy system defers work that its versioned storage depends on. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-vacuum.html">vacuum configuration reference</a> distinguishes these triggers and resource controls.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="invariants"><h2>Snapshot isolation and write skew</h2>
   <p>We have explained why a report sees a stable set of versions. That property does not automatically protect every business rule. Two transactions can each read an unchanged snapshot and make incompatible decisions while updating different rows.</p>
   <p>Consider two on-call doctors, both initially on duty. Each transaction sees two doctors and turns its own doctor off duty. Under Repeatable Read, both can commit because they changed different rows. Our two-connection test left zero doctors on duty. Nothing about choosing the correct versions for each snapshot detects the application’s “at least one remains” requirement.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL’s Serializable isolation adds tracking of dependencies between reads and writes so it can reject combinations that cannot behave like a serial execution. In the same test, one transaction failed with SQLSTATE <code>40001</code> and one doctor remained on duty. The application must retry the entire transaction from a fresh view, including the count and decision. Its earlier conclusion that two doctors were available is no longer safe to reuse.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/isolation/">isolation article</a> develops the application schedules and retry choices. Here the distinction closes the storage story: MVCC supplies the versions and snapshots, while stronger concurrency guarantees can require additional coordination or conflict detection. Vacuum then reclaims versions after every relevant use of the old view has ended.</p>
  </section>
